tr.td:
I. General Pediatric Surgery and the following pediatric surgical subspecialties on staff, in active practice, continuously available and, if needed, on-site within a timeframe that is directed by the urgency of the clinical situation:
(1) Neurosurgery;
(2) Cardiothoracic surgery;
(3) Orthopedic surgery;
(4) Plastic surgery; and
(5) Ophthalmology.

td.p:
A. All of the following equipment and supplies immediately available for existing patients and for the next potential patient:
(1) O2 analyzer, stethoscope, intravenous infusion pumps;
(2) Radiant heated bed in delivery room and available in the neonatal units;

(3) Thermal gel warming mattress;
(4) Oxygen hood with humidity;
(5) Bag and masks and/or T-piece resuscitator capable of delivering a controlled concentration of oxygen to the infant;
(6) Endotracheal tubes;

(7) Oral airways;
(8) CO2 detector;
(9) Aspiration equipment;
(10) Laryngoscope;
(11) Bowel bags/plastic wrap;
(12) Umbilical vessel catheters and insertion tray;
(13) Cardiac monitor;
(14) Pulse oximeter;
(15) Transilluminator;
(16) Phototherapy unit;
(17) Doppler blood pressure device for neonates;
(18) Cardioversion/defibrillation capability for obstetrical patients and neonates;
(19) Resuscitation equipment for obstetrical patients;
(20) Resuscitation equipment for neonates including equipment outlined in the current National Resuscitation Program:

(g) Equipment for vascular access;
(21) Individual oxygen, air, and suction outlets for obstetrical patients and neonates; and
(22) Emergency call system for both obstetrical and neonatal units as well as an emergency communication system among units.

tr.td:
D. The following medications shall be immediately available for management of obstetrical hemorrhage in the delivery area and postpartum floor:
(1) Oxytocin (Pitocin);
(2) Methylergonovine (Methergine);
(3) Misoprostol (Cytotec);
(4) Carboprost tromethamine (Hemabate); and
(5) Tranexamic acid (TXA); and

J. Written protocols and capabilities in place for the following:
(1) Assessment of risk for obstetrical hemorrhage;
(2) Maximizing accuracy in determining obstetrical blood loss;
(3) Massive transfusion;
(4) Emergency release of blood products before full compatibility testing is complete; and
(5) Management of multiple component therapy;

L. Written policy for the management of obstetrical patients with opioid use and opioid use disorder that addresses the following and other relevant issues:
(1) Universal screening of obstetrical patients for opioid use;
(2) Pharmacotherapy of the pregnant, laboring, and postpartum patient;
(3) Breastfeeding;
(4) Linkages to appropriate postpartum psychosocial support services including substance use treatment and relapse prevention program; and
(5) Reproductive health planning;

N. Written policy for optimizing post-delivery care of obstetrical patients that addresses the following and other relevant issues:
(1) Identification of postpartum patients at risk for poor health outcomes;
(2) Breastfeeding support;
(3) Linkages to appropriate medical and psychosocial services; and
(4) Reproductive health planning;
